(Bug #37 - `readable` was always 1 regardless of open mode: it tested
 fd >= 0 (true for any successfully opened handle) instead of the
 recorded open mode. `writable` already got this right, keyed off the
 handle's mode, so it is the control that pins the diagnosis - before
 the fix, writing and appending both wrongly reported readable AND
 (correctly) reported writable, which no fd-validity explanation
 survives.

 All three modes open the SAME file, so `permissions` reporting the
 same value in every row is the third control: it proves permissions
 and readable/writable are different concepts that must not be
 conflated, per LANGUAGE.md's separate entries for them.)

open a file for writing called write_handle at "./bug37_scratch.txt".
Write "hello" to write_handle.
Print "writing:".
Print write_handle's readable.
Print write_handle's writable.
Print write_handle's permissions.
Close write_handle.

open a file for appending called append_handle at "./bug37_scratch.txt".
Print "appending:".
Print append_handle's readable.
Print append_handle's writable.
Print append_handle's permissions.
Close append_handle.

open a file for reading called read_handle at "./bug37_scratch.txt".
Print "reading:".
Print read_handle's readable.
Print read_handle's writable.
Print read_handle's permissions.
Close read_handle.

Delete the file "./bug37_scratch.txt".
